   “That works,” said Mama Tete.
   Nobody had asked them to trespass. Nobody had asked them to be‐
lieve in me. They’d each been asked a plainer question: would you be
where you can listen, on ground you’re allowed to stand on, at ten o’clock our
time, when Aperture’s own sheet said the seating would start, for a person
you’ve never met?
   “What should I send them?” I asked, once the line had gone quiet.
   “An invitation,” said Mama Tete. “Not an order.”
   I wrote it myself. It took four drafts. The final one was three lines
long and used the word please twice.



On Wednesday, my mother and I bought a glass.
   I hadn’t said I would. She’d said it, in the kitchen, after I told her
what I’d done to the old one. She took her coat off its hook on a Wed‐
nesday afternoon, handed me mine, and walked me to the ninety-nine-
cent store on Fordham Road like a woman delivering a witness.
   The Pathmark set doesn’t exist anymore. The Pathmark doesn’t exist
anymore. We stood in an aisle between mop buckets and plastic
flowers, looking at a rack of drinking glasses, and she picked one up,
turned it over, inspected the rim for chips, and put it back.
   “This is going to be different from the others,” she said.
   “It’s a glass.”
   “Seven are the same. This will be a stranger.” She chose one with a
thick base and a little air bubble in the side, and held it up to the light.
“Ocho,” she said. “Ocho de familias distintas.”
   Eight, from different families.




